net/sched: sch_cake: cake_mq tuning knobs + v2 split

Follow-on to 999-718. Turns the fixed constants of the v1 split into
runtime knobs and adds the v2 split, so a setting can be explored
without a rebuild. Everything defaults to the behaviour of 999-718 (and,
for rebalance = 0, to the behaviour of the series before it).

Runtime knobs under /sys/kernel/debug/cake_mq/ :

  rebalance          0 = partage egal (defaut)
                     1 = v1 : cap d'une non saturee = servi + marge,
                         les saturees se partagent le reste
                     2 = v2 : jamais sous la part egale ; les saturees
                         recoivent en plus la part egale que les non
                         saturees n'utilisent pas
  active_hold        0 = test d'activite amont (defaut)
                     N = une instance reste active si elle a emis depuis
                         N * sync_time (lisse le compte)
  ewma_shift         lissage du debit servi, 1..8 (defaut 2)
  v1_headroom_shift  v1 : marge = servi >> shift (defaut 2, soit 25 %)
  v1_sat_shift       v1 : sature si servi >= cap - cap >> shift
                     (defaut 3, soit 7/8)
  v2_spare_pct       v2 : % de la part egale inutilisee redistribuee,
                     0..100 (defaut 100)

cake_mq/stats gains an overcommit counter: the syncs where the sum of
the local caps of the active instances exceeds the global rate, and the
excess in KiB/s. In v1 and v2 the sum need not equal the global rate at
every tick, so without this the rate conformance of a setting is not
visible; this is the counter that makes the cost of each setting
measurable rather than argued.

Ported from Segalmen's 714
(instrumentation/714-net-sched-sch_cake-mq-tuning-knobs.patch,
Segalmen/cake-mq-patches), which he stacks on his 712 and 713. Ours sits
on 999-718, the port of his 712, and inherits its three adaptations.

Two things this port changes on our tree that his patch does not, both
consequences of running on our series rather than on his:

 - The saturation criterion of our 999-714 is replaced. His 714 makes
   saturation mean "throttled with a backlog", except in v1 mode where
   the v1 criterion (throttled while serving >= 7/8 of the cap) is kept.
   With rebalance = 0 on our tree that branch is the new default, so the
   meaning of the existing "saturated" counter changes even though no
   knob is turned. The counter is not comparable across this patch.

 - In his patch that first branch cannot do what it says here. The
   measurement runs at the head of cake_dequeue, and cake_dequeue returns
   early when the instance's queue is empty ("if (!sch->q.qlen) return
   NULL;"), so sch->q.qlen is >= 1 for the whole body: "throttled with a
   backlog" is exactly "throttled". The qlen term is carried over
   verbatim rather than corrected, so that this port says what his patch
   says; the reader should treat mode 0 and mode 2 as testing overlimit
   alone. Whether to correct it belongs to Christophe, not to this port.

 - His stats block declares its struct cake_mq_stats where our 999-714
   already has one; the increments are placed in ours, at the two lines
   they follow (rate_kbs_sum, then the caps_sum overcommit test). Our
   seq_printf is extended in place rather than rewritten, so the rows our
   parsers already read keep their format.

Consequence of v2 worth stating before enabling: v2 can allocate more
than the equal share, so the sum of the local caps can exceed the global
rate, which is what the overcommit counter reports. v1 leaves the sum at
or below the global rate by construction.

Observable fact, verifiable without a rebuild: /sys/kernel/debug/cake_mq
gains active_hold, ewma_shift, v1_headroom_shift, v1_sat_shift and
v2_spare_pct (0644, defaults 0, 2, 2, 3, 100), and cake_mq/stats gains
one "overcommit ... overcommit_kbs_sum ..." row per direction. Every
knob at its default and rebalance = 0 reproduces 999-718.

Requires 999-718. Not built, not flashed. Local port only.

